WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.972 --> 00:00:04.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.710
JOHN HANSMAN: MIT has an
educational philosophy

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.360
of learning by doing.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.381
So it's fine to know about
the theoretical equations.

00:00:22.381 --> 00:00:24.130
It's quite another
thing when you actually

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.980
have to build something.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.330
And for a lot of students,
this is the first time

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:29.660
they've ever built anything.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.420
If you take RTV, it's
kind of a rubber, so--

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.044
Capstone class, it's where
you take the different things

00:00:36.044 --> 00:00:37.960
you've learned in your
undergraduate education

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.780
and pull them into something
that integrates them.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.740
In our case, we
do capstone class

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.400
looking at the design
and construction

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.087
of either an airplane
or a spacecraft.

00:00:47.087 --> 00:00:48.670
CASEY DENHAM: There's
a lot of systems

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.840
that I've gotten-- a lot
of recognition that started

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.420
with a senior capstone class.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.312
So it would always be awesome to
be the next iteration of that.

00:00:58.312 --> 00:01:01.780
PROFESSOR: 3, 2, 1,
and we're flying.

00:01:06.171 --> 00:01:07.670
BOB SHIN: These
innovative solutions

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:12.260
the students come up with, it
is a challenging problem, that's

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:13.130
really unique.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:14.970
And that's what I
suppose is really value.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.810
STUDENT: How do we,
like, connect to i?

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.640
TONY TAO: You don't really build
much in the first semester,

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.639
and you're not meant to.

00:01:19.639 --> 00:01:22.590
The idea is for everyone to come
in to work with their models,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.320
to come up with
ideas and sketches.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.230
And in the spring, you
take the old designs

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:28.580
and you iterate on
them experimentally.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.612
And then you build them, and
you try to get the thing to fly.

00:01:31.612 --> 00:01:33.070
SHANELA CLARKE: We
wanted to create

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:37.270
an unmanned aerial vehicle along
with a submersible system that

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.590
will be used to either conduct
reconnaissance or search

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.412
and rescue missions in
blue-water operations.

00:01:42.412 --> 00:01:44.620
JOHN HANSMAN: The project
was sponsored by the Office

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:45.520
of Naval Research.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.502
And the motivation was
to be able to resupply

00:01:48.502 --> 00:01:52.142
a UAV in deep water when
you're away from land.

00:01:52.142 --> 00:01:53.850
SHANELA CLARKE: When
I first heard of it,

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.254
I was like, oh, wow,
this is so cool.

00:01:56.254 --> 00:01:58.295
STUDENT: One of the major
problems with seaplanes

00:01:58.295 --> 00:02:01.560
is that they can dig
their pontoons in and flip

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.544
upon landing.

00:02:03.544 --> 00:02:04.960
JOHN HANSMAN: The
students came up

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.729
with a relatively ingenious idea
of having the pontoons rotate

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.830
so that if the airplane
did end up on its back,

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:13.538
it wouldn't be like
a turtle on its back.

00:02:13.538 --> 00:02:16.260
It would be able to
get flipped over.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.050
STUDENT: There is a worm gear
system inside the airplane that

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.980
tilts the pontoons so it kind
of does an upside down push-up.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.680
Then the body actually is
lifted out of the water,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.420
and then the body is then
free to rotate around.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.670
JOHN HANSMAN: There's
a very strong software

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.770
in autonomy component.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:38.410
So the pod knows where it is
with its local GPS system.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.690
So it broadcasts a
message to the airplane,

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.010
I'm at this location.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.460
The airplane comes and lands
close to that location.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.520
They use an optical
camera that was

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.640
sensitive to certain colors.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.890
They put colored
markers on the pod

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.440
so that the optical
system would know to look

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.870
for the signature of the pod.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.580
That sounds pretty
easy, but it actually

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.170
gets tough because now
you're in rough water.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:04.780
So the pod isn't still.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:05.760
It's going up and down.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.620
The airplane's being
bounced around.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.635
So it has to find
the target, and it

00:03:10.635 --> 00:03:13.010
has to sort of average out
where the target's going to be

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.134
and head in that direction.

00:03:14.134 --> 00:03:16.820
As it gets closer and closer,
it gets a better and better fix.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:23.916
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:03:23.916 --> 00:03:25.790
PROFESSOR: Is someone
recording the settings?

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:26.282
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:03:26.282 --> 00:03:28.032
PROFESSOR: Okay, good,
I didn't know that.

00:03:28.032 --> 00:03:28.734
Ready?

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:29.234
All right.

00:03:29.234 --> 00:03:29.734
Ready?

00:03:33.162 --> 00:03:33.662
All right.

00:03:33.662 --> 00:03:34.162
Full power.

00:03:40.577 --> 00:03:42.410
TONY TAO: Pontoons are
submarining at speed,

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.457
and I don't have
enough Q on the tail

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:45.790
to be able to bring the nose up.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:46.290
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:46.790
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:48.070
TONY TAO: And we couldn't
get the airplane to take off.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.236
We tried a couple different
angles and flap settings

00:03:50.236 --> 00:03:51.870
and couldn't get it to take off.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.300
Diagnosed it as the
pontoons submarining down

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.230
into the water.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:58.710
Did a quick fix with that, just
mixed up some glue and pieces

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.869
of plywood to try to get
the pontoons to sit up

00:04:00.869 --> 00:04:01.535
a little better.

00:04:01.535 --> 00:04:06.181
STUDENT: Takeoff attempt 2.2.

00:04:06.181 --> 00:04:09.139
STUDENT: Whoo, whoo!

00:04:09.139 --> 00:04:12.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:16.147 --> 00:04:18.230
TONY TAO: You take it to
the field and you try it.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.329
And if it doesn't
work, you take video

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:22.600
and you take
measurements, and you

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.656
use that to refine your design.

00:04:25.656 --> 00:04:27.530
JOHN HANSMAN: First
flight test, first crash.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.482
It's sort of the way it
goes in this business.

00:04:30.482 --> 00:04:30.982
STUDENT: OK.

00:04:30.982 --> 00:04:32.476
Let me try it.

00:04:32.476 --> 00:04:35.706
It's got a little too
much here to start with.

00:04:35.706 --> 00:04:37.580
DENNIS PRIETO: It didn't
actually get to fly,

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:42.811
but we did learn what we need
to be able to fly basically.

00:04:42.811 --> 00:04:45.504
We hadn't done enough
surface area attachment

00:04:45.504 --> 00:04:46.920
on the inside of
the carbon fiber.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.020
So the legs actually
sheared, and we

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.610
weren't able to
take off properly

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:52.936
on the final take-off attempt.

00:04:52.936 --> 00:04:54.310
And then before
that, we realized

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:56.476
that our tail needed to be
a little bit bigger so we

00:04:56.476 --> 00:04:58.367
could have more nose
control, nose authority.

00:04:58.367 --> 00:05:00.200
And then those are some
of the modifications

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.360
that we made so far,
and hopefully it'll

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:03.550
fly on Wednesday.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:04.464
Awesome.

00:05:04.464 --> 00:05:05.880
JOHN HANSMAN:
They're figuring out

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.360
how things that look good on
paper, theoretical models,

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.890
work in the real world.

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:14.530
They're understanding that you
have to make approximations.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.680
You have to use
engineering judgment.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.160
You have to decide
what's important

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.230
and what's not important.

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:20.910
STUDENT: Oh, no.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.090
They glued it in wrong.

00:05:24.777 --> 00:05:26.610
TONY TAO: After the
flight test at the pond,

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.260
we made a bunch of
changes to the aircraft.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:30.887
First of all, the
biggest change is

00:05:30.887 --> 00:05:33.470
that we took the tail, which was
here and which was too small,

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:35.070
gave it a bigger
tail and put it here

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:37.630
in the prop wash of the motors.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:41.340
And we included these scoops
at the front of the pontoons

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.060
to help with the pitch
stability and the submarining

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:45.095
problem on the water.

00:05:45.095 --> 00:05:47.220
And all these design changes
have been carried over

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.180
onto the new aircraft,
which has these hooks here

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.910
to allow the water to
separate and for it

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:54.180
to build up high pressure.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.880
That keeps the pontoons from
nose-diving into the water.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.860
And this one has also been
given a molded Kevlar fuselage

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.390
to improve the aerodynamics
and keep everything inside.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.930
SHANELA CLARKE: What's special
about the capstone process

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:15.685
is the fact that it's really
simulating the engineering

00:06:15.685 --> 00:06:16.185
environment.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.280
BOB SHIN: Many of the students
describe their experience

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.210
as transformational.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.160
They become fearless.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:34.785 --> 00:06:35.410
MAN: All right.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:36.390
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.370
MAN: Automatic.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:38.953
Everybody, it's in
automatic mode now.

00:06:43.944 --> 00:06:45.860
TONY TAO: The fact that
they get to experience

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.030
being both an engineer
and a technician, somebody

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:50.920
who actually is the one who puts
these things together, I think,

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:52.586
makes them better
engineers because they

00:06:52.586 --> 00:06:56.690
can learn to understand how
their designs could actually

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:57.190
be built.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:00.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

